---
import Base from '../layouts/base.astro'
import { privacyFacts as f } from '../lib/privacy-facts.ts'
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Free Planning Poker stores about your team, what it measures and how, how long everything is kept, and how to have it deleted."
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p>
    This page says in plain words what Free Planning Poker stores, what it measures and for how long. There is no
    account, no cookie and no consent banner. The full source code is on
    <a href="https://github.com/jkrumm/free-planning-poker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>, so
    every claim here can be checked.
  </p>
  <p><small>Last updated: 22 November 2026</small></p>

  <section id="summary">
    <h2>In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A room keeps what a team estimated, not who estimated it. History names a seat by its number, never by name.</li>
      <li>Nothing is written to your device by our measurement. No cookie, no identifier from us.</li>
      <li>No advertising, no third-party trackers, no external analytics service.</li>
      <li>Visitors are counted with a keyed hash of an anonymous device id and a date, and that is deleted after {f.visitorRetention.words}.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="who">
    <h2>Who runs the service</h2>
    <p>
      The person named in the <a href="/imprint">imprint</a> runs Free Planning Poker and is responsible for the data
      described here. The fastest way to reach them about privacy is the <a href="/contact">contact form</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rooms">
    <h2>What a room stores</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The room.</strong> Its settings, its display name, its topics (the titles and links you type or
        import, with a tracker reference if you import one), and its rounds with their results: the cards each seat
        played, the spread and the agreed value. Rounds and results are kept; no job deletes them by age.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No names in history.</strong> History records a vote under the seat's number at the table. Who sat
        there is known only to the live state.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The live state.</strong> Who sits where, their display names and the votes not yet revealed are kept
        so a room survives a reconnect or a restart. They expire {f.snapshotTtl.words} after the room's last event.
        A vote leaves the server only after the reveal; before it, only its own seat sees it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The address.</strong> A room is reached by a number, and by a name if its team books one. A number is
        held for {f.numberHold.words} after the room's last visit and a booked name for {f.nameHold.words}; every
        visit renews the hold. After that the address can go to another room. The room's rows stay.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The access code of a locked room</strong> is stored readable and shown to every seat. It keeps
        strangers out; it is not a secret between the people in the room. Do not reuse a password for it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Guessing the code</strong> is slowed down: after {f.accessAttempts.words}, further attempts are
        refused for a minute.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="counting">
    <h2>Counting visitors</h2>
    <p>
      To know how many people use the tool, the server writes one row per device and day on which that device cast a
      vote. The row holds a date and a keyed hash of the anonymous device id your browser made up. The key is a
      secret on our server, so a copy of the database cannot be matched to a browser. Rows are deleted after
      {f.visitorRetention.words}. The same tables feed the public statistics.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="measurement">
    <h2>What is measured</h2>
    <p>
      We run Umami, an analytics tool, on our own server. Its script is loaded from our own address under
      <code>/api/u</code>, so your browser contacts no other host for it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No cookie, nothing stored on your device,</strong> and no identifier from us. Your device id is not given to it.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>What it receives</strong> for each page view: the page path, the referring page, the page title, your
        screen size and your browser language. Your IP address is used in memory to derive a coarse location and an
        anonymous session value; it is not stored.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Room addresses are rewritten</strong> to <code>/room/:slug</code>, and query strings and fragments are
        dropped, so neither a room name nor an access code reaches it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Actions it counts:</strong> the landing page button, a room created, a room joined (with a size
        bucket of the table), an invite link copied, the first vote cast in a room, a round revealed (with a bucket
        of votes and whether the team agreed), a topic decided (and how), a room locked, a summary exported, the
        app installed and an embedded room loaded. These never carry a vote value, a display name or a room address.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Its session</strong> rotates {f.umamiSession.words}: someone who comes back after the month is a new
        session to it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Why there is no banner.</strong> The measurement sets no cookie, keeps nothing on your device and builds
      no profile of you, so we run it without asking. That is our reading of the law; some data-protection
      authorities read it more strictly, because the script reads screen size and language from your browser. If you
      would rather not be counted, block requests to <code>/api/u</code> in your browser; the app works the same
      without it, and no product number depends on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Error reports.</strong> The pages report uncaught errors and changes of the room connection (such as
      reconnecting) to our own server, with a random session id kept in memory for {f.errorSession.words}. They carry
      no vote, display name, room name, topic title, tracker key or access code. There is no screen recording and no
      measurement of how fast the page was for you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="device">
    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      Your browser's local storage holds the following, all under keys that start with <code>fpp:v9:</code>. Nothing
      is a cookie, and none of it is sent anywhere except what a room needs to seat you.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>profile</code>: your name and avatar.</li>
      <li><code>prefs</code>: sound, calm mode, view, theme and language.</li>
      <li><code>seat:&lt;room&gt;</code>: the secret that proves your seat in that room.</li>
      <li>
        <code>recent</code>: the rooms you visited, with address, display name, creation time, the access code of a
        locked room, and whether it is pinned. You can remove each one; an unpinned one is dropped after
        {f.recentDropped.words} without a visit.
      </li>
      <li><code>device</code>: the anonymous device id behind the count above.</li>
      <li><code>stats</code>: your personal record, which stays on the device.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On your first visit after the move from the earlier version, its stored name, preferences, recent room and
      device id are read once and copied into these keys. Clearing your browser's site data removes all of it; you
      lose your seats and your recent rooms with it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact-form">
    <h2>The contact form</h2>
    <p>
      The form sends the email address you type, a subject, and your name and message if you add them, to our server.
      Our own email service in Germany receives it and passes it to our inbox through Resend (Resend, Inc., USA),
      which acts as our processor under a data processing agreement and the EU standard contractual clauses. We use
      it only to answer you. We do not store messages in the application's database and never use them for marketing.
      <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Resend privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hosting">
    <h2>Who handles your requests</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> serves the marketing pages and sits in front of the whole site, so it sees the
        technical data of every request, such as your IP address and the address you ask for, as any host does.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Our own server</strong> in Nuremberg, Germany runs the room, the API, the database, the measurement and
        the error reports. All transfers are encrypted. No third-party analytics or monitoring service is involved.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="basis">
    <h2>Legal basis</h2>
    <p>
      Running the service, counting visitors and measuring use rest on our legitimate interest in understanding and
      improving the tool and keeping it secure and reliable (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). The data is minimal and does not
      identify you directly. The contact form rests on taking steps at your request and answering your enquiry
      (Art. 6(1)(b) and (f) GDPR).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="deletion">
    <h2>Having something deleted</h2>
    <p>
      Ask through the <a href="/contact">contact form</a>. By hand we can delete a room: send its address, and its
      topics, rounds and votes go with it. We can delete the counting rows of a device if you send us the value stored
      under <code>fpp:v9:device</code> in your browser, because with it we can compute the hash. We cannot tie a
      vote or a seat to a person: names exist only in the live state, which expires on its own.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rights">
    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you have the right to access your data, to have it rectified or erased, to restrict or object to
      its processing, and to data portability, as well as the right to withdraw any consent you have given. Because we
      keep nothing that identifies a person directly, we may be unable to find data about you without help from you;
      we act on every genuine request.
    </p>
    <p>
      You may also lodge a complaint with a data protection supervisory authority. The one responsible for us is the
      Bavarian State Office for Data Protection Supervision (Bayerisches Landesamt für Datenschutzaufsicht, BayLDA),
      Ansbach, Germany,
      <a href="https://www.lda.bayern.de" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">www.lda.bayern.de</a>. You can
      also contact the authority of your habitual residence.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
